method:
Face types by ancestry group and sex, from modern photographs (1995 on) of film, television and music stars and models widely described as beautiful or handsome, found on Wikimedia Commons under public domain, CC0, CC BY or CC BY-SA licences (measured only, never copied; each credited in sources).

- one photograph a person: alone in the frame, |yaw| <= 22 deg, the face at least 120 px wide, the eyes open; checked by eye (no glasses, hats, veils, microphones or hands over the face, no drawings);
- MediaPipe face landmarks, frontalised, centred on the eyes' centres and scaled to put them one unit apart; the upper face averaged over every photograph, below the nose tip over calm (closed, unsmiling) mouths;
- each type fitted in the landmark model's own space: the face rendered (no makeup, hair off the face), read the same way, its vertices moved by half the remaining difference (x and y), symmetrised and smoothed over 4 mm; eight iterations, the best kept; lid margins and the inner lip line left out;
- bone (per group): brow ridge, nasal root and cheekbone depth from the stars' photographs against the Northern European stars; nasal bridge, tip and lips from soft-tissue norms and estimates;
- variants: each type's stars split in two by face shape (k-means on the outline, brows, eye corners, nose and mouth corners; four people or more each), the cluster's difference added, at most 5 mm.
